# skills/test-strength/targets/floor.target
# Subject: the Bash guard. Checker: floor-equiv replaying the real corpus.
#
# WHY NOT the floor suite (tests/floor/). 6583 lines then, minutes-scale, and this box has no
# `timeout` to bound a hang — a per-class sweep is hours on one serialised
# runner, and a gate too expensive to run gets stood down. floor-equiv is the
# NARROWEST command that exercises the mutated thing and it brings its own
# 676-payload corpus. Measured 2026-08-30: 16.1s per run at --limit 30, and a
# guard replaced by `exit 0` comes back "11 identical, 19 DIFFERENT", exit 1.
#
# KNOWN AND STATED: floor-equiv is blind in one direction by construction. Every
# corpus payload was selected because the BASELINE blocks it, so the tool sees a
# candidate that LOSES a block and cannot see one that ADDS a block. That makes
# it the right instrument for `threshold-widen`, `guard-clause-delete` and
# `exit-neuter`, and the WRONG one for `threshold-narrow`, whose whole effect is
# to add blocks. NARROW is declared anyway and its survivors are expected: a
# survivor that is the instrument's known blind spot is a finding about the
# instrument, and deleting the class would hide it.
#
# The two controls are what close that blind spot from the other side: they fire
# the PRISTINE guard on ordinary work and require an ALLOW.

target        floor
subject-kind  posix-shell
lane          deep
cost          20
sites         3
seed          repo:bin/guard-bash.sh
stage         copy-tree repo:bin $WORK/base
stage         copy-tree repo:bin $WORK/cand
stage         mkdir $WORK/cfg
place         $WORK/cand/guard-bash.sh
checker-cmd   sh bin/floor-equiv.sh --baseline $WORK/base --candidate $WORK/cand --corpus fixtures/guard-corpus.jsonl --limit 30 --config $WORK/cfg
green         0
red           1

mutate  CMP     comparison-flip
mutate  WIDE    threshold-widen
mutate  NARROW  threshold-narrow
mutate  GUARD   guard-clause-delete
mutate  EXIT    exit-neuter
mutate  NEG     negation-drop
mutate  ALT     alternation-drop
mutate  MSG     message-drop

manual  TCC-1   a macOS TCC denial makes a guard look busy rather than blocked; no textual mutation of the guard reproduces it
manual  SHAPE-1 floor-equiv reports only the rule NAME, so a refactor moving a decision from crontab-install.flat to crontab-install.argv is verdict-identical here; a shape-level mutation needs an instrument this target does not have

# `cmd` controls fire the PRISTINE guard on a legitimate payload and require an
# ALLOW. Constitution No.2 rule 11: a guard that blocks ordinary work gets
# switched off, and a switched-off guard protects nothing.
control LEGIT-RSYNC cmd CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR=$WORK/cfg sh $SUBJECT < $TDIR/floor/legit-rsync.json
        varies: shape
        why:    `rsync -a --delete ./build/ host:/srv/` is ordinary deploy work and carries a --delete the destructive rules match on
control LEGIT-FIND  cmd CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR=$WORK/cfg sh $SUBJECT < $TDIR/floor/legit-find-delete.json
        varies: lexical
        why:    `find build -name '*.tmp' -delete` carries every token a destructive-op rule looks for and is still ordinary work

# DECLARED AND ABSENT ON THIS BOX. cc-audit covers a different axis entirely —
# malicious payloads in a skill — and naming it here is how a reader learns that
# axis was not measured, rather than assuming this run covered it.
scanner cc-audit cc-audit scan $WORK/cand/guard-bash.sh
